NNAAMMEE
     ssttrriinnggss — print the strings of printable characters in files

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssttrriinnggss [--aa | ----aallll] [--ee _e_n_c_o_d_i_n_g | ----eennccooddiinngg==_e_n_c_o_d_i_n_g]
             [--ff | ----pprriinntt--ffiillee--nnaammee] [--hh | ----hheellpp]
             [--nn _n_u_m_b_e_r | ----bbyytteess==_n_u_m_b_e_r | --_n_u_m_b_e_r] [--oo]
             [--tt _r_a_d_i_x | ----rraaddiixx==_r_a_d_i_x] [--vv | ----vveerrssiioonn] [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     For each _f_i_l_e specified, the ssttrriinnggss utility prints contiguous sequences
     of printable characters that are at least _n characters long and are fol‐
     lowed by an unprintable character.  The default value of _n is 4.  By de‐
     fault, the ssttrriinnggss utility only scans the initialized and loaded sections
     of ELF objects; for other file types, the entire file is scanned.  The
     ssttrriinnggss utility is mainly used for determining the contents of non-text
     files.

     If no file name is specified as an argument, standard input is read.

     The following options are available:

     --aa | ----aallll
             For ELF objects, scan the entire file for printable strings.

     --ee _e_n_c_o_d_i_n_g | ----eennccooddiinngg==_e_n_c_o_d_i_n_g
             Select the character encoding to be used while searching for
             strings.  Valid values for argument _e_n_c_o_d_i_n_g are:
             _s       for single 7-bit-byte characters (ASCII, ISO 8859).
             _S       for single 8-bit-byte characters.
             _l       for 16-bit little-endian.
             _b       for 16-bit big-endian.
             _L       for 32-bit little-endian.
             _B       for 32-bit big-endian.
             The default is to assume that characters are encoded using a sin‐
             gle 7-bit byte.

     --ff | ----pprriinntt--ffiillee--nnaammee
             Print the name of the file before each string.

     --hh | ----hheellpp
             Print a usage summary and exit.

     --nn _n_u_m_b_e_r | ----bbyytteess==_n_u_m_b_e_r | --_n_u_m_b_e_r
             Print the contiguous character sequence of at least _n_u_m_b_e_r char‐
             acters long, instead of the default of 4 characters.  Argument
             _n_u_m_b_e_r should specify a positive decimal integer.

     --oo      Equivalent to specifying --tt _o.

     --tt _r_a_d_i_x | ----rraaddiixx==_r_a_d_i_x
             Print the offset from the start of the file before each string
             using the specified radix.  Valid values for argument _r_a_d_i_x are:
             _d       for decimal
             _o       for octal
             _x       for hexadecimal

     --vv | ----vveerrssiioonn
             Display a version identifier and exit.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ssttrriinnggss utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To display strings in _/_b_i_n_/_l_s use:
           $ strings /bin/ls

     To display strings in all sections of _/_b_i_n_/_l_n use:
           $ strings -a /bin/ln

     To display strings in all sections of _/_b_i_n_/_c_a_t prefixed with the filename
     and the offset within the file use:
           $ strings -a -f -t x /bin/cat

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     ar(1), nm(1), objdump(1), ranlib, readelf(1), size(1)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The first FreeBSD ssttrriinnggss utility appeared in FreeBSD v3. It was later
     discontinued in FreeBSD v5, when i386-only a.out format was dropped in
     favor of ELF.

